{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Chalmers assumes the system can do Peano arithmetic and uses that capacity to construct G. Lean instead assumes diagonalization directly, for every formula, in a universe-0 language with decidable symbol equality and codes for its own sentences. It thus covers settings without full PA that still supply the required fixed point."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Chalmers uses one assertion relation throughout. Lean requires the proofs supplied by (1)–(3), as well as the fixed-point equivalence, in T₀, while (4) and the conclusion concern T. This can be more demanding for a fixed weaker T₀, but every T₀ theorem transfers to T, and taking T₀ = T recovers the single-system formulation."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean models unassailable assertion as classical first-order derivability and allows B to be any unary formula satisfying the stated laws, rather than a designated belief symbol. Its interpretation as belief is external to the formal statement."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The target captures the contradiction: given (1)–(3) and the fixed-point resources, if T proves ¬B(⊥) as in (4), then T is inconsistent. The coding requirements concern syntax; neither theory nor the belief predicate is required to be recursively enumerable."
}
